    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Has a Taste of the e-MTB World with the RN0 Motor-Assisted Bicycle\nBlack-on-Black Temerario Comes From Lamborghini Palm Beach To Twist Your Bank Account\n10 photos\nPhoto: Instagram | lambopalmbeach\nLamborghini offers plenty of personalization options for its new-age lineup consisting of plug-in hybrids only. These include the Urus SE Performante , the Revuelto (and Revuelto SV), and the Temerario, in case you forgot.\nThe list becomes even longer if you factor in the Raging Bull’s personalization division, Ad Personam, which pretty much renders a chunk of the tuning game useless. Sure, it may not offer wacky body kits, but the customization is definitely interesting, and if you are looking for various colors inside and out, then you can find most (or almost all) combinations in the official catalog.\nAt first glance, the Lamborghini Temerario pictured in the gallery may look like it came from a renowned tuner’s portfolio. But it hasn’t, as despite the stealthy look, it is bone stock. In fact, Lamborghini Palm Beach recently dropped these images on its social media, accompanied by a few words. And to be honest, we do not know whether this example is for sale, or if it is nothing more than a simple marketing tool, but it is so cool that it will probably make some consider sprucing up their lives with one of these.\nPhoto: Instagram | lambopalmbeach The supercar in question features a Nero Noctis exterior. Th glossy black paint was applied to all body panels, as well as the wheels, and the only contrasting element on the outside lies behind the alloys. We’re talking about the lime green brake calipers with Lamborghini branding. The interior is decorated with a few red touches, and it consists of black leather upholstery, as well as a few suede inserts, and it is definitely something to write home about.\nSince this particular Lamborghini Temerario hasn’t been touched with the tuning stick, it still has 907 hp (920 ps/677 kW) available via the right pedal. That’s how much the three electric motors and the twin-turbocharged 4.0-liter V8 engine produce in total. The PHEV setup allows the brand’s new “baby” supercar to reach 62 mph (100 kph) in 27 seconds, and a 213 mph (343 kph) top speed.\nNow, if this supercar has made you crave a Lamborghini Temerario of your own, then you should know that it starts at roughly $380,000 for the 2027 model year, before destination, options, and dealer fees.\nLamborghini’s Temerario entered production in 2025 at the Sant’Agata Bolognese factory. It “lent” its PHEV setup and other nuts and bolts to the Audi Nuvolari, and it is the Huracan’s successor. Some have criticized it for looking a bit bland compared to its larger sibling, the Revuelto , but to be honest, we (or I, in this case) think that Lambo has definitely nailed the design, as this is one head-turning machine. But would you rather have one of these or a barely-driven Huracan instead? View this post on Instagram A post shared by Lamborghini Palm Beach (@lambopalmbeach)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nlamborghini temerario Lamborghini temerario supercar PHEV v8 USA\nAbout the author: Cristian Gnaticov\nAfter a series of unfortunate events put an end to Cristian's dream of entering a custom built & tuned old-school Dacia into a rally competition, he moved on to drive press cars and write for a living. He's worked for several automotive online journals and now he's back at autoevolution after his first tour in the mid-2000s. Full profile\nUP NEXT Spacecraft Reaches Asteroid NASA Hit Hard in 2022 for the First-Ever Space Crash Site Investigation",
